WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
And get started. I'll call the meeting to order at seven.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:05.920
Thank you, everybody.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.800
We'll state the Pledge of Allegiance.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.680
I pledge allegiance to the flag

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.040
of The United States

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.945
>> Of America and to the republic for

00:00:16.825 --> 00:00:18.825
which it stands one nation

00:00:19.785 --> 00:00:20.825
under God

00:00:20.985 --> 00:00:22.345
indivisible

00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:23.465
with liberty

00:00:23.545 --> 00:00:24.585
and justice

00:00:24.825 --> 00:00:25.545
all.

00:00:25.785 --> 00:00:26.505
>> Thank you.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.300
All right,

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.980
referring to our agenda for tonight. First item is public speak, do we have any public speak.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.325
Hearing none, I guess let's move on to item three, which is additions and deletions

00:00:43.485 --> 00:00:45.405
of the agenda items.

00:00:45.805 --> 00:00:48.365
Anybody have any additions or deletions to the agenda?

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:54.900
Okay,

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:04.180
hearing none, we'll move on to item number four, which is the town administrator's report. Eric, the floor is yours. Okay, Amanda, can I share my screen here?

00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:07.905
Can you give me the power and the glory?

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:12.385
You've got the power!

00:01:12.865 --> 00:01:13.585
Okay.

00:01:13.745 --> 00:01:17.665
>> Glory is up to you Eric. Let's see if I can figure out which one it is.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:19.825
Yes.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.220
Okay, apologize to not getting you this,

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.620
at a reasonable time. Are you seeing a Word document?

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.340
Yep. Yeah with the graph. Yep. Okay.

00:01:33.305 --> 00:01:37.785
>> So first things first of the things that are going to affect us,

00:01:38.425 --> 00:01:42.105
as you probably are well aware, the state is,

00:01:42.985 --> 00:01:44.905
spiking pretty dramatically

00:01:44.905 --> 00:01:45.705
on COVID.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.090
I've been plotting day by day what our infection rate

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:55.690
is the fourteen day rolling average of infection and that's what this graph is.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.130
We had a spike in mid October.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:03.175
We actually broke the red, you know, fifteen cases,

00:02:03.655 --> 00:02:04.455
you know,

00:02:07.095 --> 00:02:08.935
per day per 100,000

00:02:08.935 --> 00:02:09.975
population

00:02:10.295 --> 00:02:11.735
back in October,

00:02:11.975 --> 00:02:19.140
but just barely and then we immediately went back down. We had a kind of, you know, temporary spike over about a week and a half.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.260
Then it went down to baseline and

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.740
since about the November

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.540
9,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.940
it's been accelerating pretty dramatically.

00:02:29.085 --> 00:02:30.845
We're a small town

00:02:31.005 --> 00:02:34.525
so our data is always going to look really choppy

00:02:34.685 --> 00:02:39.405
because a few people here, a few people there cause big spikes in our data.

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:40.285
But

00:02:40.525 --> 00:02:43.245
the bottom line is we're about double

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.040
the threshold for a town to be in the red zone as far as the state goes.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.160
They consider anything over 15 you

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.160
know to to be their trigger

00:02:56.495 --> 00:02:59.695
and we're at about 29 as of today.

00:03:00.335 --> 00:03:05.455
So that's pretty much where we're at. Don't know where we're going but you

00:03:05.455 --> 00:03:07.855
know it's not particularly promising.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.450
So

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.970
we are going to do some changes at the town level.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:15.330
Immediately

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:20.370
after this Thursday, we're going to close the town hall to the public.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.355
Instead of being appointment only, it will be closed,

00:03:25.355 --> 00:03:27.755
it will be town hall employees only.

00:03:27.995 --> 00:03:31.595
If people need to meet with somebody, it will be in the,

00:03:33.995 --> 00:03:36.520
you know, the all purpose, the community room.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.200
As most of you know, the governor has reverted the state back to phase 2.1.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.320
And then there were a bunch of other executive orders and DPH guidelines.

00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:57.765
So the thing that has made life difficult is that now if you are going to do in person meetings

00:03:58.725 --> 00:04:00.725
you also have to provide

00:04:00.885 --> 00:04:03.125
basically remote access

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:04.570
even if you're

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.930
scheduling an in person meeting because anybody

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.770
can request that.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:15.210
So it's making in person meetings much more difficult because you have to have the capability

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.210
to do a hybrid meeting.

00:04:19.475 --> 00:04:21.155
So that's kind of annoying.

00:04:21.315 --> 00:04:28.115
There is a public town meeting that will be scheduled in January, it got pushed back from December

00:04:28.515 --> 00:04:29.395
to

00:04:29.395 --> 00:04:30.435
accept

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:34.330
a donation of actually three parcels not two parcels

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:41.290
but it's essentially revenue neutral to the town and they're being donated they're not being

00:04:43.415 --> 00:04:56.215
you know we're not paying for them so it's not something the Board of Finance has to really weigh in on I'm just letting you know and they're all basically orphaned open space parcels so the tax implications

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.630
on one of them is like $92

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:04.630
a year in revenue and the other one's in a non profit so we're not getting anything from it anyway.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:05.670
There's

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.430
no real issue

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:08.150
with us.

00:05:08.995 --> 00:05:09.795
Obviously,

00:05:09.795 --> 00:05:12.515
we held an election. The election went really well.

00:05:12.755 --> 00:05:15.475
The turnout in Andover was fantastic.

00:05:15.475 --> 00:05:17.075
We had an 87%

00:05:17.075 --> 00:05:19.475
turnout of eligible voters,

00:05:19.875 --> 00:05:22.355
so that was pretty cool, and we had no issues.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:29.340
We did have to employ a hell of a lot more poll workers than we normally do,

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:37.435
because the state requirements had kind of gone through the roof and we actually also chewed up a fair bit of money

00:05:37.675 --> 00:05:41.355
doing the prep work dealing with all the absentee ballots

00:05:41.355 --> 00:05:44.475
because those are a super time intensive

00:05:45.995 --> 00:05:46.555
thing

00:05:46.875 --> 00:05:55.020
you know based on the state guidelines for what you have to do. So we're okay budget wise now but if we

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:02.940
get into budget season in the spring and we have municipal elections too, I suspect we're going to be over

00:06:04.515 --> 00:06:10.755
in that line item. Wally was very he's told me you know almost since July

00:06:10.915 --> 00:06:15.875
that he thought he anticipated that there was no way they were going to stay within that budget.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:32.200
So but you know it depends on how many issues we have, how many town meetings we have, what do we actually have coming up because that budget tends to fluctuate a fair bit and what the absentee ballot situation is for

00:06:32.275 --> 00:06:33.235
the

00:06:33.235 --> 00:06:35.555
town elections in the spring.

00:06:35.875 --> 00:06:37.395
That's still an unknown.

00:06:38.755 --> 00:06:40.435
So public works,

00:06:40.435 --> 00:06:43.955
the road crew foreman Ed has officially retired

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.210
as of December 1,

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:53.570
we did agree to meet him and pay him out an additional portion of his sick leave

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.930
to kind of make that happen in an orderly way.

00:06:57.545 --> 00:07:00.345
So which you know is not ideal,

00:07:00.985 --> 00:07:02.825
but on the other hand,

00:07:03.225 --> 00:07:08.665
Ed maxed out his three hundred days of sick leave many years ago

00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:11.305
and has been giving all the excess days back.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:14.230
So from a financial standpoint,

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:16.950
yes, it's definitely a hit to the town,

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.270
but you know to

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.110
make the transition

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.670
to a true public works supervisor,

00:07:25.955 --> 00:07:29.315
you know, that's what we ended up having to do.

00:07:30.675 --> 00:07:37.235
We have a position open for both the maintainer one and the public work supervisor.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.690
We're kind of negotiating a little bit now

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:42.610
around that.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.250
So we'll see. I mean,

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:50.450
I will be honest in that what we were paying Ed compared to

00:07:51.425 --> 00:07:53.905
what the job responsibilities

00:07:53.905 --> 00:07:55.345
were asking for

00:07:55.905 --> 00:07:57.505
are very different.

00:07:57.905 --> 00:07:58.545
So

00:07:58.785 --> 00:08:00.865
there's going to be some increase.

00:08:00.865 --> 00:08:02.465
We're going to have to increase that.

00:08:03.185 --> 00:08:04.225
Exactly how much,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.190
I don't know. That's gonna be a discussion,

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:10.550
you know, going forward.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:15.270
But it's definitely it's gonna cost us some money that's for sure.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:26.455
We're still doing road work. We still got some driveway aprons to complete after the paving operations we've done in the last month,

00:08:26.935 --> 00:08:31.255
and we're still continuing to fill potholes from time to time.

00:08:32.055 --> 00:08:33.895
We're talking about instituting

00:08:33.895 --> 00:08:34.775
a

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:36.340
weather service

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:40.020
that's tailored just to public works departments.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:44.260
We're looking at one. There's one other we may consider also.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.580
The costs are fairly reasonable given,

00:08:47.175 --> 00:08:53.095
you know, if it makes you make better judgment and you can use public works more efficiently

00:08:53.735 --> 00:08:56.695
by having a more accurate weather forecasting,

00:08:57.095 --> 00:08:59.095
it's probably financially worth it.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:04.680
You know, the plans run from about a thousand bucks to about $1,500

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.920
depending on exactly how much info

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.520
and what bells and whistles you want. Eric?

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.440
>> Yes. Quick quick question. This is some private service that

00:09:14.965 --> 00:09:18.405
supposedly gives us better weather forecasting than we can get

00:09:18.805 --> 00:09:19.685
for free?

00:09:21.445 --> 00:09:22.245
>> Yes.

00:09:22.725 --> 00:09:24.085
Yes. Because

00:09:24.725 --> 00:09:26.005
you want

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.900
kind of different things

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:31.140
than the average person.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:44.615
You know, one of the things you want is you want to forecast what the road temperatures are gonna be, because that's critical to how much salt you put down and when your weather snow is gonna stick, whether it's not gonna stick,

00:09:45.015 --> 00:09:47.175
you know, and you want much more accurate.

00:09:50.615 --> 00:09:53.575
You know, there's a bunch of things that you want

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:55.420
tailored specifically

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:56.700
to a location.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:00.060
You know I've dealt with

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.540
professional weather services

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:05.420
in the sailing

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:12.665
you know we use them for weather forecasting for you know picking locations of events and accurately forecasting

00:10:13.065 --> 00:10:14.025
snowfall

00:10:14.025 --> 00:10:16.025
and things of that nature. So

00:10:17.145 --> 00:10:21.945
you know I've seen it be a huge advantage to me in the planning stage.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:26.540
It's almost more a question of which service you use whether

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:31.020
as opposed to whether you should do it or not at least in my book.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:36.860
And we'd probably just pay for it out of town Aid Road so you wouldn't see it in the actual budget anyway.

00:10:38.015 --> 00:10:41.055
Okay. It's not all, still tax dollars.

00:10:42.415 --> 00:10:47.375
We're still up in the air over the closing out the Times Farm Bridge project.

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:50.975
As most of you know, that was the bridge project from hell.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.570
It was initially started about 2000,

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:56.010
design

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:01.530
and then dropped because the town didn't really want to pay for it to actually do it and then

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.210
eventually the

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:13.525
DOT basically said either you start building it, rebuilding it or we're going to condemn it and you know nobody's going to be driving over it. So then they started again

00:11:13.765 --> 00:11:17.125
it got completed in about 2017,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:20.120
but

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.640
there was never

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:23.720
a final clearance

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:25.800
and

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:28.440
it's

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.840
chewed up an enormous amount of my time,

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:34.600
but basically what's happened is the

00:11:34.435 --> 00:11:36.915
both staff members that were our

00:11:37.955 --> 00:11:39.475
registered professionals

00:11:39.475 --> 00:11:42.195
on the job at CME Engineering

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:45.075
left the company to pursue other opportunities.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:53.510
They basically just about closed out the project, but not quite. We got all the final approvals from the state,

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:55.430
but they're still

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:58.070
arguing about

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.710
one change order that occurred

00:12:01.215 --> 00:12:04.335
in like the summer of twenty seventeen.

00:12:04.575 --> 00:12:06.735
And it's about a $17,000

00:12:06.735 --> 00:12:07.615
discrepancy

00:12:07.615 --> 00:12:08.335
between

00:12:11.855 --> 00:12:12.815
the contractor

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:17.870
and the engineering firm that has to certify the expenses.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:22.430
So we would be on the hook for just 20% of that $17,000

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.430
and we have the money sitting in that account,

00:12:25.085 --> 00:12:34.365
but I actually couldn't even pay the extra money right now just to close it out because then the state wouldn't reimburse me for any of it. So,

00:12:35.085 --> 00:12:43.910
we're just sitting on it, you know, hopefully we'll resolve that in the next couple of months, but I know I've been saying that since I took over as the administrator

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:49.030
and I heard Joe say that for two years his entire time in office so

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.630
maybe it really will be in a couple of months but you know who knows.

00:12:53.485 --> 00:12:55.085
We did get

00:12:55.485 --> 00:12:56.925
a steep grant,

00:12:57.565 --> 00:12:59.325
we were notified

00:12:59.485 --> 00:13:00.925
on the ninth

00:13:01.005 --> 00:13:07.005
just before the Board of Selection meeting that we had gotten the steep grant for paving and reclaiming

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:14.200
a section of Long Hill Road as well as a section of Shawty Mill Road. So that's about $128,000

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:16.760
added to the paving budget that we didn't have.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:18.520
So that's good news.

00:13:19.855 --> 00:13:21.695
The connectivity grant

00:13:21.855 --> 00:13:22.495
is

00:13:23.695 --> 00:13:28.895
I'm still struggling trying to get the state to give me

00:13:29.855 --> 00:13:30.735
a document,

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:37.870
which is really annoying. It is just a letter saying that at some time in the future they will give me an encroachment permit.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:42.510
An encroachment permit is necessary anytime you do construction

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.270
within the right of way

00:13:47.845 --> 00:13:49.045
of a state road.

00:13:50.005 --> 00:13:51.365
Basically because

00:13:51.845 --> 00:13:58.485
there's another construction project going on there at the same time, which is the upgrade of the actual signal lights,

00:13:59.845 --> 00:14:02.405
they didn't want to issue it because

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:03.920
the

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:10.160
encroachment permit and the work that was being done at the intersection at the time were coming out of two different divisions

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:12.560
in DOT and

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:19.255
the connectivity grant is being run by a whole different division so you have basically three divisions of

00:14:20.295 --> 00:14:24.935
DOT which aren't really communicating very well with each other. It's kind of annoying.

00:14:25.335 --> 00:14:28.135
Here's the bottom line and the unfortunate part.

00:14:28.375 --> 00:14:30.415
We've got until December

00:14:30.415 --> 00:14:37.010
31 to sign a contract with the contractor that we picked to do the actual construction

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:39.330
because we're using a

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:44.370
basically an accelerated construction program called EZIQC

00:14:44.645 --> 00:14:47.205
that is run by the

00:14:49.285 --> 00:14:51.765
Capital Regional Council of Governments

00:14:52.645 --> 00:14:54.645
did the initial bid,

00:14:55.045 --> 00:15:00.070
you know, package for it. So the good news from us is

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:03.430
the DOT really likes those projects

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:13.005
run through EZ IQC because it makes their lives easier. And since it's not tax dollars, it's the DOT's dollars anyway, if they like the program,

00:15:13.565 --> 00:15:16.445
I'm happy to use it. The downside

00:15:16.445 --> 00:15:20.605
is if we don't have an agreement in place by December 31,

00:15:21.085 --> 00:15:21.725
then

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:23.290
they're renegotiating

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:27.690
the contracts for next year and depending on who the contractor that's chosen,

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.410
I may have to start over with a

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.770
lot of the design

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:38.335
bidding, which would really annoy me. But you know,

00:15:40.415 --> 00:15:43.055
still no reason why that shouldn't be done

00:15:43.615 --> 00:15:44.495
starting

00:15:44.655 --> 00:15:54.580
early spring, early to mid spring next year. It's just a question of whether I have to work really hard in spring or whether I just have to sign it by December 31.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:59.380
But there's two things that have to be released before then and I don't know whether that'll happen.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:01.780
Questions on that?

00:16:03.635 --> 00:16:06.355
Sorry, I know that's mostly irrelevant.

00:16:07.235 --> 00:16:17.395
Town hall, we're still in the planning stages for figuring what would have to happen if we did have to shut down the town hall again and send employees home.

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:19.870
I'm hoping we don't do that,

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:21.950
but we're

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:28.110
spending a small amount of money that's in our IT budget to equip us with a few new laptops

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.510
to kind of make that process a little smoother.

00:16:32.665 --> 00:16:36.345
You know, as you know, the COVID count is rising pretty rapidly.

00:16:36.665 --> 00:16:39.545
So it may be something we have to deal with

00:16:39.865 --> 00:16:41.065
at some point.

00:16:43.305 --> 00:16:47.705
We are also implementing a program called Security Mentor,

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:52.080
which is basically an online training program

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:53.600
in information

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:54.400
security,

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:58.400
which and cyber security, which I think as you all know

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:00.640
is kind of important

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:01.360
for the town

00:17:02.515 --> 00:17:05.955
given some of the things we've experienced

00:17:06.755 --> 00:17:08.515
in the last few months.

00:17:08.835 --> 00:17:10.035
So we're

00:17:10.035 --> 00:17:14.595
in the process of implementing that. I think it's a relatively low cost program.

00:17:15.075 --> 00:17:18.930
It's you know about $9 a year per employee

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.850
that participates.

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:21.730
So

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:26.530
we're doing that and we are also we've

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:28.690
you know essentially hired

